<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<aiml version="2.0">

    <!--
      Recommendation / "which mutation should I pick" categories. Phrased as
      first-person advice. Each ends with [[FOLLOWUPS]] suggesting two or
      three concrete next questions, all of which exist in the brain.
    -->

    <category>
        <pattern>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[That depends on what you are trying to learn about your protocol. Here is the short version:

• You want to know which steps are essential → Skip.
• You want to know whether an extra message would leak something → Add.
• You suspect a value is interchangeable with another of the same shape → Replace.
• You want to test message-piggybacking instead of new round-trips → Combine.
• You want to model state loss or stress-test redundancy → Forget.
• You want to audit skipped checks in real implementations → Neglect.

Tell me your scenario in one line and I will narrow it down.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] Which mutation should I pick for my scenario? | I want to test for leaks | I want to test redundancy | I want to test type confusion]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>WHICH MUTATION</pattern><template><srai>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>RECOMMEND A MUTATION</pattern><template><srai>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT MUTATION SHOULD I USE</pattern><template><srai>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHICH ONE SHOULD I USE</pattern><template><srai>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>HELP ME PICK A MUTATION</pattern><template><srai>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT SHOULD I PICK</pattern><template><srai>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHICH MUTATION SHOULD I PICK FOR MY SCENARIO</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Describe the scenario in one sentence — for example:

• "I want to know if dropping the third message breaks authentication."
• "I want to check whether replacing the nonce with another agent's nonce lets the run finish."
• "I want to model a phone losing the session key mid-handshake."

Once I have that, I will pick the mutation (or pair of mutations) that actually answers it.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] I want to test for leaks | I want to test redundancy | I want to test type confusion]]></template>
    </category>

    <category>
        <pattern>I WANT TO TEST FOR LEAKS</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[For leak detection, start with Add. It injects an extra Send, so if any secret leaves the role through the new message, the analysis will surface it.

If the suspected leak is implementation-side (a log line, a debug echo), follow up with Combine Only — it tests piggybacking the value onto an existing message, which is what such leaks usually look like in real code.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Add? | What is Combination Only? | Which mutation should I pick?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>HOW DO I TEST FOR LEAKS</pattern><template><srai>I WANT TO TEST FOR LEAKS</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>CHECK FOR LEAKS</pattern><template><srai>I WANT TO TEST FOR LEAKS</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>I WANT TO TEST REDUNDANCY</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[For redundancy checks, Forget is the right tool. Mark the value you suspect of being redundant as forgotten and run the analysis — if the run completes without that value, you have proved redundancy; if downstream derivations get blocked, the value was essential.

Skip is the lighter alternative when you care about whole steps being redundant rather than individual values.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Forget? | What is Skip? | What is a blocked derivation?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>HOW DO I TEST REDUNDANCY</pattern><template><srai>I WANT TO TEST REDUNDANCY</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>I WANT TO TEST TYPE CONFUSION</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Replace Type is built for this. It swaps a value for another of the same type — exactly the kind of substitution a type-confusion attacker would exploit. If the goal still holds, your protocol binds to identity, not type. If it fails, the role is treating "any T" as good enough.

Replace Sub Messages is a useful complement when you want to pinpoint which field is doing the work.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Replace Type? | What is Replace Sub Messages? | What is Replace?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>HOW DO I TEST TYPE CONFUSION</pattern><template><srai>I WANT TO TEST TYPE CONFUSION</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT SHOULD I RUN FIRST</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Run Skip first. It is the cheapest mutation and gives you a quick map of which steps actually carry security weight. After that, you can target Forget or Replace on the specific values used by those steps.

The slowest mutations are Forget with infinite-depth derivation — leave those for last, once you know where to point them.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Skip? | When should I use Forget? | Which mutation should I pick?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>WHERE SHOULD I START</pattern><template><srai>WHAT SHOULD I RUN FIRST</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT TO TRY FIRST</pattern><template><srai>WHAT SHOULD I RUN FIRST</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHICH MUTATION IS FASTEST</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Skip is fastest by a wide margin — it only removes a step, so the mutant theory is barely larger than the original. Add and Replace come next. Combine is similar to Add. Forget is the slowest, especially with infinite-depth derivation, because the analysis has to enumerate alternative derivation paths for every blocked rule.

For a quick first sweep, Skip everything; for a thorough audit, end with Forget.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Skip? | What is Forget? | What should I run first?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHICH MUTATION IS SLOWEST</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Forget, with the infinite-depth derivation option. It explores every alternative path the agent could use to reconstruct a forgotten value, and that search space grows quickly. Limited-depth Forget is much faster and is usually enough.

If a Forget run is taking too long, drop to Limited Depth or set a specific depth via the Depth field.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Forget? | What is a blocked derivation? | Which mutation is fastest?]]></template>
    </category>

</aiml>
